es LLC\n685: ​\n686: ​\n687: ​\n688: ​\n689: B-\n690: 1\n691: ​\n692: ​\n693: Annex C   Section 262 of the Delaware General Corporation Law\n694: ​\n695: ​\n696: ​\n697: ​\n698: C-\n699: 1\n700: ​\n701: ​\n702: ii\n703: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n704: ​\n705: SUMMARY\n706: This summary discusses material information contained in this proxy statement, including with respect to the merger agreement, the merger and the other agreements entered into in connection\n707: with the merger. We encourage you to read carefully this entire proxy statement, its annexes and the documents referred to or incorporated by reference in this proxy statement, as this\n708: Summary may not contain all of the information that may be important to you. The items in this Summary include page references directing you to a more complete description of that topic in\n709: this proxy statement.\n710: Unless stated otherwise or the context otherwise requires, in this proxy statement, all references to:\n711: •\n712: “PetSmart,” the “Company,” “we,” “our,” or “us” refer to PetSmart, Inc., a Delaware corporation;\n713: ​\n714: •\n715: “Parent” refers to Argos Holdings Inc., a Delaware corporation and “Merger Sub” refers to Argos Merger Sub Inc., a Delaware corporation;\n716: ​\n717: •\n718: the “Buyer Group” refers to a consortium of funds advised by BC Partners, Inc., La Caisse de dépôt et placement du Québec, affiliates of GIC Special Investments Pte Ltd, affiliates of\n719: StepStone Group LP and, at all times after December 12, 2014, Longview Asset Management, LLC;\n720: ​\n721: •\n722: “common stock” refers to the Company’s common stock, par value $0.0001 per share;\n723: ​\n724: •\n725: the “board” refers to the board of directors of the Company;\n726: ​\n727: •\n728: the “merger agreement” refers to the Agreement and Plan of Merger, dated as of December 14, 2014, as it may be amended from time to time, by and among the Company, Parent and Merger Sub, a\n729: copy of which is included as Annex A to this proxy statement;\n730: ​\n731: •\n732: the “merger” refers to the merger of Merger Sub with and into the Company, with the Company continuing as the surviving corporation; and\n733: ​\n734: •\n735: the “SEC” refers to the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission.\n736: ​\n737: The Parties to the Merger Agreement (page 16)\n738: PetSmart, Inc.\n739: PetSmart, Inc. is a Delaware corporation. PetSmart is one of the largest providers of pet products and services in North America. PetSmart sells goods and services in 1,404 stores and\n740: operates 202 in-store dog and cat boarding facilities in the United States, Canada and Puerto Rico. See “\n741: The Parties to the Merger Agreement — PetSmart, Inc.\n742: ”\n743: Additional information about PetSmart is contained in our public filings with the SEC, which are incorporated by reference herein. See “\n744: Where You Can Find Additional Information.\n745: ”\n746: Argos Holdings Inc. and Argos Merger Sub Inc.\n747: Argos Holdings Inc. is a Delaware corporation. Argos Merger Sub Inc. is a Delaware corporation and a wholly owned subsidiary of Parent. Parent and Merger Sub are currently affiliates of\n748: funds advised by BC Partners, Inc. (“BC Partners”) and at the closing of the transactions contemplated by the merger agreement will be owned by the Buyer Group. Both Parent and Merger Sub\n749: were formed solely for the purpose of entering into the merger agreement and consummating the transactions contemplated by the merger agreement, and have not engaged in any business except\n750: for activities incidental to their formation and as contemplated by the merger agreement. See “\n751: The Parties to the Merger Agreement — Argos Holdings Inc. and Argos Merger Sub Inc.\n752: ”\n753: The Special Meeting (Page 17)\n754: The special meeting will be held at the Sheraton Crescent Hotel, 2620 W. Dunlap Avenue, Phoenix, AZ 85021, on March 6, 2015, at 1:00 p.m., local time.\n755: 1\n756: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n757: Proposals to be Voted on at the Special Meeting (page 17)\n758: At the special meeting, you will be asked to consider and vote upon the following proposals:\n759: •\n760: the proposal to adopt the merger agreement (the “merger proposal”);\n761: ​\n762: •\n763: the proposal to approve, on an advisory (non-binding) basis, specified compensation that may be paid or become payable to the named executive officers of the Company in connection with the\n764: merger (the “compensation proposal”); and\n765: ​\n766: •\n767: the proposal to approve the adjournment of the special meeting from time to time, if necessary or appropriate, to solicit additional proxies if there are insufficient votes at the time of\n768: the special meeting to approve the proposal to adopt the merger agreement (the “adjournment proposal”).\n769: ​\n770: Effect of Merger (Page 21)\n771: The merger agreement provides that at the effective time of the merger each outstanding share of common stock (other than shares owned by the Company, any subsidiary of the Company, Parent,\n772: any direct or indirect holding company of Parent, Merger Sub and holders who are entitled to and properly exercise appraisal rights under Delaware law), will be converted into the right to\n773: receive $83.00 in cash (the “merger consideration”). Following the merger, the Company will therefore become a privately held company, wholly owned by Parent and the Company’s common stock\n774: will be delisted from NASDAQ and deregistered under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, as amended. At the closing of the transactions contemplated by the merger agreement, Parent will be\n775: owned by the Buyer Group.\n776: Record Date and Quorum (Page 17)\n777: The holders of record of the common stock as of the close of business on January 29, 2015 (the record date for determination of stockholders entitled to notice of and to vote at the special\n778: meeting), are entitled to receive notice of and to vote at the special meeting. As of the close of business on the record date, there were 99,455,151 shares of common stock outstanding.\n779: The presence at the special meeting, in person or by proxy, of the holders of a majority of shares of common stock outstanding as of the close of business on the record date will constitute\n780: a quorum. Under our bylaws, in the absence of a quorum at the special meeting, the meeting of stockholders may be adjourned, but no other business shall be transacted.\n781: Required Vote (Page 18)\n782: For the Company to complete the merger, under Delaware law, stockholders holding at least a majority of the shares of common stock outstanding at the close of business on the record date\n783: must vote “\n784: FOR\n785: ” the merger proposal. In addition, under the merger agreement, the receipt of such required vote is a condition to the consummation of the merger. A failure to vote your shares of common\n786: stock or an abstention from voting will have the same effect as a vote against the merger proposal.\n787: The compensation proposal and the adjournment proposal each requires the affirmative vote of holders of a majority of the shares of common stock present in person or represented by proxy at\n788: the meeting and entitled to vote thereon.\n789: Voting Support Agreement (Page 45)\n790: On December 14, 2014, the Company entered into a voting agreement (the “voting agreement”) with Parent and Longview Asset Management, LLC (“Longview”), on behalf of Longview clients and\n791: certain related parties. Under the voting agreement, Longview agreed, among other things, to vote or cause to be voted 7,424,591 shares of common stock, which represent approximately 7.5% of\n792: the total outstanding shares of common stock as of the close of business on the record date, in favor of the adoption of the merger agreement, and against any other action, proposal,\n793: agreement or transaction made in opposition to or competition with the merger or the merger agreement that is not approved by the board. The voting agreement will terminate upon the earliest\n794: to occur of  (i) the effective time of the merger, (ii) the termination of the merger agreement in accordance with its terms, (iii) the termination of the voting\n795: ~~~ PAGE 3 ~~~\n796: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n797: agreement by the mutual written consent of the Company, Parent and Longview, (iv) a change of recommendation (as defined under “\n798: The Merger Agreement — Other Covenants and Agreements — No Solicitation\n799: ) or (v) the making of any change, by amendment, waiver or other modification to any provision of the merger agreement that decreases the amount of, or changes the form of, the merger\n800: consideration.\n801: Conditions to the Merger (Page 69)\n802: Each party’s obligation to complete the merger is subject to the satisfaction or waiver of the following conditions:\n803: •\n804: the adoption of the merger agreement by the required vote of the stockholders;\n805: ​\n806: •\n807: the absence of any U.S. or Canadian order or law that prohibits or otherwise makes illegal the consummation of the merger; and\n808: ​\n809: •\n810: the expiration or termination of any applicable waiting period under the Hart-Scott-Rodino Antitrust Improvements Act of 1976, as amended (the “HSR Act”) and the Competition Act (Canada), as\n811: amended, including the regulations promulgated thereunder (the “Competition Act”).\n812: ​\n813: The obligation of Parent and Merger Sub to complete the merger are subject to the satisfaction or waiver of the following additional conditions:\n814: •\n815: the accuracy of the representations and warranties of the Company (subject to materiality, material adverse effect and other qualifications specified in the merger agreement);\n816: ​\n817: •\n818: the Company’s performance of and compliance with its obligations and covenants under the merger agreement in all material respects; and\n819: ​\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 820]","title":"read","metadat