{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824958302,"sessionID":"ses_f461f6897ffeZJz46cQwZkb8iB","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9e17eb4001Z4KRtyxLtMGlX3","sessionID":"ses_f461f6897ffeZJz46cQwZkb8iB","messageID":"msg_0b9e17915001FqJyYUCCEmwT4C","type":"tool","id":"call_00_woNsrkYRqBxBt6DZpclF7054","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/tmp/opencode/petsmart/paged.txt","offset":820,"limit":280},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/petsmart/paged.txt, lines 820-1099\n820: •\n821: the delivery of an officer’s certificate by the Company certifying that the conditions in the two bullets above have been satisfied.\n822: ​\n823: The obligation of the Company to complete the merger is subject to the satisfaction or waiver of the following additional conditions:\n824: •\n825: the accuracy of the representations and warranties of Parent and Merger Sub (subject to materiality, knowledge and other qualifications specified in the merger agreement);\n826: ​\n827: •\n828: Parent’s and Merger Sub’s performance of and compliance with their obligations and covenants under the merger agreement in all material respects; and\n829: ​\n830: •\n831: the delivery of an officer’s certificate by Parent certifying that the conditions in the two bullets above have been satisfied.\n832: ​\n833: When the Merger Becomes Effective (Page 53)\n834: We anticipate completing the merger in the first half of 2015, subject to the adoption of the merger agreement by the Company’s stockholders as specified herein and the satisfaction of the\n835: other closing conditions.\n836: Recommendation of the Company’s Board of Directors (Pages 17 and 30)\n837: The board unanimously (i) determined that the merger agreement and the merger are advisable and in the best interests of the Company and its stockholders, (ii) approved the execution,\n838: delivery and performance of the merger agreement, and (iii) resolved to recommend the adoption of the merger agreement by the stockholders of the Company and directed that such matter be\n839: submitted for consideration of the stockholders of the Company at the special meeting. The board unanimously recommends that the stockholders of the Company vote “\n840: FOR\n841: ” the merger proposal, “\n842: FOR\n843: ” the compensation proposal and “\n844: FOR\n845: ” the adjournment proposal. For a description of the reasons considered by the board in deciding to recommend approval of the merger proposal, see “\n846: The Merger (Proposal 1) — Reasons for the Merger.\n847: ”\n848: ~~~ PAGE 4 ~~~\n849: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n850: Opinion of J.P. Morgan (Page 30 and Annex B)\n851: At the meeting of the board on December 13, 2014, J.P. Morgan Securities LLC (“J.P. Morgan”) rendered its oral opinion, subsequently confirmed in writing on December 14, 2014, to the board\n852: that, as of such date and based upon and subject to the factors, assumptions and limitations set forth in its opinion, the merger consideration to be paid to the holders of the Company’s\n853: common stock in the merger was fair, from a financial point of view, to such holders.\n854: The full text of the written opinion of J.P. Morgan dated December 14, 2014, which sets forth the assumptions made, procedures followed, matters considered and limitations on the review\n855: undertaken in rendering its opinion, is attached as Annex B to this proxy statement and is incorporated herein by reference. The Company urges stockholders to read the opinion in its\n856: entirety. J.P. Morgan’s written opinion is addressed to the board, is directed only to the merger consideration paid to the holders of the Company’s common stock in the merger and does not\n857: constitute a recommendation to any stockholder of the Company as to how such stockholder should vote with respect to the merger or any other matter. The summary of the opinion of J.P. Morgan\n858: set forth in this proxy statement is qualified in its entirety by reference to the full text of such opinion.\n859: Treatment of Company Stock Options and Company RSU Awards (Page 45)\n860: Company Options\n861: .   Except as otherwise agreed in writing between any holder and Parent, each option to purchase a share of common stock (a “Company option”) whether vested or unvested, that is outstanding\n862: immediately prior to the effective time of the merger will, as of the effective time of the merger, become fully vested and be converted into the right to receive an amount in cash, less\n863: applicable tax withholdings, equal to the product obtained by multiplying (a) the excess, if any, of the merger consideration over the exercise price per share of the Company option, by (b)\n864: the total number of shares subject to the Company option.\n865: Company RSU Awards\n866: .   Except as otherwise agreed in writing between any holder and Parent, each award of restricted stock units that corresponds to shares of common stock (a “Company RSU award”) whether\n867: vested or unvested, that is outstanding immediately prior to the effective time of the merger, will, as of the effective time of the merger, become fully vested and be converted into the\n868: right to receive an amount in cash, less applicable tax withholdings, equal to (1) the product obtained by multiplying (a) the total number of shares subject to the Company RSU award, by (b)\n869: the merger consideration, plus (2) any dividends accrued with respect to the Company RSU award.\n870: Company PSU Awards\n871: .   Except as otherwise agreed in writing between any holder and Parent, each performance stock unit award that corresponds to shares (a “Company PSU award”) that is outstanding immediately\n872: prior to the effective time of the merger will become fully vested (based on actual performance, in the case of awards whose performance period ends prior to the effective time of the\n873: merger, at 109.6% of target levels, in the case of awards granted in the 2013 fiscal year, and at 150.0% of target levels, in the case of awards granted in the 2014 fiscal year) and be\n874: converted into the right to receive an amount in cash, less applicable tax withholdings, equal to (1) the product obtained by multiplying (a) the total number of shares subject to the\n875: Company PSU award by (b) the merger consideration, plus (2) any dividends accrued with respect to the Company PSU award.\n876: Company Restricted Stock\n877: .   Except as otherwise agreed in writing between any holder and Parent, effective as of immediately prior to the effective time of the merger, each then-outstanding restricted share of\n878: common stock (the “Company restricted stock”) will automatically become fully vested and the restrictions thereon will lapse, and each such share of Company restricted stock will be\n879: cancelled and converted into the right to receive an amount in cash, less applicable tax withholdings, equal to the merger consideration, plus any dividends accrued with respect to the\n880: Company restricted stock.\n881: Interests of the Company’s Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger (Page 45)\n882: In considering the recommendation of the board with respect to the merger proposal, you should be aware that some of the Company’s directors and executive officers have interests in the\n883: merger that are different from, or in addition to, the interests of the Company’s stockholders generally. Interests of officers and directors that may be different from or in addition to the\n884: interests of the Company’s stockholders include, among others:\n885: ~~~ PAGE 5 ~~~\n886: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n887: ​\n888: •\n889: The merger agreement provides for the accelerated vesting and cash-out of all outstanding Company equity awards.\n890: ​\n891: •\n892: The Company’s executive officers are participants in the Company’s Executive Change in Control and Severance Benefit Plan, which provides for enhanced severance benefits in the event of a\n893: qualifying termination of employment within three months prior to or 36 months following the completion of the merger.\n894: ​\n895: •\n896: Pursuant to the merger agreement, the Company has established a cash-based retention program to promote retention and to incentivize efforts to consummate the merger under which certain\n897: executive officers have been granted awards.\n898: ​\n899: •\n900: Under the Company’s 2005 Deferred Compensation Plan, all matching contributions will vest and participants will be entitled to receive full accelerated payment of their plan balances upon\n901: the occurrence of a change in control.\n902: ​\n903: •\n904: The Company’s directors and executive officers are entitled to continued indemnification and insurance coverage under indemnification agreements and the merger agreement.\n905: ​\n906: These interests are discussed in more detail in the section entitled “\n907: The Merger (Proposal 1) — Interests of the Company’s Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger\n908: ”. The board was aware of the different or additional interests set forth herein and considered such interests along with other matters in approving the merger agreement and the transactions\n909: contemplated thereby, including the merger.\n910: Financing (Page 40)\n911: Parent estimates that the total amount required to complete the merger and related transactions and pay related fees and expenses will be approximately $8.4 billion. Parent expects this\n912: amount to be funded through a combination of the following:\n913: •\n914: debt financing in an aggregate principal amount of up to approximately $6.2 billion as well as a $750 million senior secured asset-based revolving credit facility, a portion of which will be\n915: available at closing. Parent has received firm commitments from a consortium of financial institutions to provide the debt financing and revolving credit facility (see “\n916: The Merger (Proposal 1) — Financing — Debt Financing\n917: ”);\n918: ​\n919: •\n920: cash equity investments by members of the Buyer Group (other than Longview) in an aggregate amount up to approximately $1.83 billion and Longview’s contribution to Parent of  $250 million\n921: worth of the Company’s common stock immediately prior to the effective time of the merger. Parent has received equity commitments for the equity financing from the Buyer Group (other than\n922: Longview) and has entered into a rollover agreement with Longview with respect to its contribution of Company common stock to Parent (see “\n923: The Merger (Proposal 1) — Financing —  Equity Financing\n924: ”); and\n925: ​\n926: •\n927: approximately $425 million of cash is expected to be on hand in the Company and available at the closing.\n928: ​\n929: The consummation of the merger is not subject to a financing condition (although the funding of the debt and cash equity financing is subject to the satisfaction of the conditions set forth\n930: in the commitment letters under which the financing will be provided).\n931: Termination Fee Commitment Letters (Page 44)\n932: Each of the members of the Buyer Group (other than Longview) has entered into a termination fee commitment letter with Parent and the Company and has agreed on a several basis, and subject\n933: to other terms and conditions of the termination fee commitment letters, to pay to the Company (or if consented to by the Company, to purchase common equity securities of Parent to enable\n934: Parent to pay to the Company) an amount equal to its portion of the $510 million Parent termination fee and certain expense reimbursement that may be payable by Parent under the merger\n935: agreement, if and when due under the merger agreement. See “\n936: The Merger — Termination Fee Commitment Letters\n937: .”\n938: ~~~ PAGE 6 ~~~\n939: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n940: Material U.S. Federal Income Tax Consequences of the Merger (Page 49)\n941: The receipt of cash in exchange for shares of common stock pursuant to the merger will generally be a taxable transaction for U.S. federal income tax purposes. You should consult your own\n942: tax advisors regarding the particular tax consequences to you of the exchange of shares of common stock for cash pursuant to the merger in light of your particular circumstances (including\n943: the application and effect of any state, local or foreign income and other tax laws).\n944: Regulatory Approvals (Page 52)\n945: Under the HSR Act and related rules, certain transactions, including the merger, may not be completed until notifications have been given and information furnished to the Antitrust Division\n946: of the United States Department of Justice (“Antitrust Division”) and the Federal Trade Commission (“FTC”) and all statutory waiting period requirements have been satisfied or early\n947: termination has been granted by the applicable agencies. On December 24, 2014, both the Company and Parent filed their respective Notification and Report Forms with the Antitrust Division\n948: and the FTC. The 30-day waiting period under the HSR Act will expire at 11:59 pm on January 23, 2015, unless it is terminated early or otherwise extended. Both the Company and Parent\n949: received early termination of the HSR waiting period on January 7, 2015.\n950: The merger is also conditioned on the applicable waiting period under the Competition Act having expired or been terminated by the applicable government agencies. Under the Competition Act,\n951: the parties must file a pre-merger notification and observe the specified waiting period requirements before consummating the merger, unless the parties are exempted from such requirements\n952: through the issuance of an Advance Ruling Certificate (an “ARC”), or a “no-action” letter together with a waiver of the notification and waiting period requirements. On December 30, 2014,\n953: the parties submitted a request to the Commissioner of Competition in Canada for an ARC or, in the alternative, a “no-action” letter. The parties were issued an ARC pursuant to section 102\n954: of the Competition Act on January 14, 2015.\n955: No Solicitation (Page 61)\n956: Under the merger agreement, the Company and its affiliates and their respective representatives may not:\n957: •\n958: solicit, initiate or knowingly encourage or facilitate the making or submission of an alternative proposal (defined below under\n959: The Merger Agreement — Other Covenants and Agreements — No Solicitation\n960: ) or any inquiries, discussions or offers that constitute or could reasonably expected to lead to any alternative proposal;\n961: ​\n962: •\n963: participate in any discussions or negotiations regarding an alternative proposal with, or furnish any nonpublic information regarding the Company or its subsidiaries in connection with an\n964: alternative proposal to, any person that has made, or is considering making an alternative proposal (except to notify such person as to the non-solicitation provisions of the merger\n965: agreement); or\n966: ​\n967: •\n968: enter into any letter of intent, agreement in principle, merger or acquisition agreement or any other agreement relating to or providing for any alternative proposal (except for\n969: confidentiality agreements permitted under the merger agreement).\n970: ​\n971: But if, prior to obtaining the required vote of the Company’s stockholders to adopt the merger agreement, the Company receives an unsolicited alternative proposal from a third party that in\n972: the good faith judgment of the board, after consultation with outside legal and financial advisors, constitutes, or could reasonably be expected to result in, a “superior proposal” (defined\n973: below under\n974: The Merger Agreement — Other Covenants and Agreements — No Solicitation\n975: ), then the Company may take the following actions:\n976: •\n977: provide nonpublic information to the third party after entering into a confidentiality agreement acceptable under the terms of the merger agreement; and\n978: ​\n979: •\n980: engage in discussions or negotiations with a third party with respect to the alternative proposal.\n981: ​\n982: ​\n983: ~~~ PAGE 7 ~~~\n984: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n985: In order for the board to change its recommendation in response to a superior proposal or for the Company to terminate the merger agreement to accept a superior proposal, the Company must\n986: (among other obligations):\n987: •\n988: provide Parent written notice of the board’s intention to make a change in recommendation or to terminate the merger agreement with a description of the terms of the superior proposal;\n989: ​\n990: •\n991: provide to Parent in writing its reasons for making the change in recommendation or for terminating the merger agreement; and\n992: ​\n993: •\n994: provide Parent with three business days to propose changes to the terms of the merger agreement (which will be extended for additional two business day periods for any material change in the\n995: superior proposal), and during this period negotiate with Parent in good faith to make adjustments to the terms and conditions of the merger agreement.\n996: ​\n997: Following the end of such three business day period (and any extension thereto), after taking into account any revisions to the terms and conditions of the merger agreement proposed by\n998: Parent, the board must determine that the superior proposal remains superior to the merger proposal. If the Company terminates the merger agreement to enter into an agreement with a third\n999: party regarding a superior proposal or the board changes its recommendation in response to a superior proposal and the agreement is terminated by Parent, then the Company must pay a\n1000: termination fee of  $255 million to Parent (as described in more detail under\n1001: The Merger Agreement — Termination\n1002: ).\n1003: Termination (Page 70)\n1004: The Company and Parent may terminate the merger agreement by mutual written consent at any time before the effective time of the merger. In addition, either the Company or Parent may\n1005: terminate the merger agreement if:\n1006: •\n1007: the effective time of the merger has not occurred on or before June 14, 2015 (the “end date”), and the party seeking to terminate the merger agreement pursuant to this provision has not\n1008: breached in any material respect its obligations under the merger agreement in any manner that has contributed to the failure to consummate the merger on or before such date and has used the\n1009: efforts required of it to consummate the merger (including obtaining antitrust approvals) and to obtain the proceeds of the financings;\n1010: ​\n1011: •\n1012: a governmental entity of competent jurisdiction has issued or entered an injunction or similar order permanently enjoining or otherwise prohibiting the consummation of the merger and such\n1013: injunction has become final and non-appealable, so long as the party seeking to terminate the merger agreement pursuant to this provision has not breached in any material respect its\n1014: obligations under the merger agreement in any manner that has contributed to such injunction or order and has used the efforts required of it under the merger agreement to prevent, oppose\n1015: and remove such injunction or order; or\n1016: ​\n1017: •\n1018: the Company’s stockholders fail to adopt the merger agreement after the conclusion of a stockholder meeting (including any adjournments or postponements thereof) held for that purpose.\n1019: ​\n1020: The Company may terminate the merger agreement:\n1021: •\n1022: if Parent or Merger Sub has breached or failed to perform in any material respect any of its representations, warranties, covenants or other agreements contained in the merger agreement\n1023: which would result in the failure to satisfy a closing condition or would result in the failure of the closing to occur, subject to a 30 business day cure period if the breach or failure is\n1024: capable of being cured, so long as the Company is not then in material breach of any representation, warranty, agreement or covenant contained in the merger agreement (a “Parent Breach\n1025: Termination”);\n1026: ​\n1027: •\n1028: at any time prior to the Company stockholder meeting to accept a “superior proposal” (defined below under\n1029: The Merger Agreement — Other Covenants and Agreements — No Solicitation\n1030: ) so\n1031: ​\n1032: ​\n1033: ~~~ PAGE 8 ~~~\n1034: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1035: long as (i) prior to or substantially concurrent with such termination, the Company has paid a termination fee of  $255 million to Parent and (ii) promptly after such termination, the\n1036: Company enters into a definitive agreement with respect to such superior proposal; or\n1037: •\n1038: if  (i) the merger is not consummated by the second business day after the first date upon which Parent was required to consummate the closing and (ii) the Company was ready, willing, and\n1039: able to consummate the merger and provided written notice to Parent confirming such fact (a “Parent Failure to Close Termination”).\n1040: ​\n1041: Parent may terminate the merger agreement:\n1042: •\n1043: if the board takes an action that constitutes a change of recommendation (as defined below under\n1044: The Merger Agreement — Other Covenants and Agreements — No Solicitation\n1045: ) prior to the stockholder meeting to adopt the merger agreement; or\n1046: ​\n1047: •\n1048: if the Company breached or failed to perform in any material respect any of its representations, warranties, covenants or other agreements contained in the merger agreement which would\n1049: result in the failure to satisfy a closing condition or would result in the failure of the closing to occur, subject to a 30 business day cure period if the breach or failure is capable of\n1050: being cured, so long as Parent or Merger Sub is not then in material breach of any representation, warranty, agreement or covenant contained in the merger agreement.\n1051: ​\n1052: Termination Fees (Page 71)\n1053: The Company will pay to Parent a termination fee of  $255 million in the event that:\n1054: •\n1055: the Company has terminated the merger agreement before stockholder approval of the merger proposal is obtained in response to a superior proposal and promptly after such termination the\n1056: Company enters into a definitive agreement with respect to such superior proposal;\n1057: ​\n1058: •\n1059: Parent has terminated the merger agreement in the event of a change of recommendation; or\n1060: ​\n1061: •\n1062: the merger is terminated under specified conditions and concurrently with or within 12 months after such termination, the Company enters into a definitive agreement providing for a\n1063: qualifying transaction (as defined below under “\n1064: The Merger Agreement — Termination Fees\n1065: ”) or completes a qualifying transaction.\n1066: ​\n1067: Parent will pay to the Company a reverse termination fee of  $510 million in cash in the event that:\n1068: •\n1069: the Company has terminated the merger agreement as a result of a Parent Breach Termination or a Parent Failure to Close Termination; or\n1070: ​\n1071: •\n1072: the Company or Parent has terminated the merger agreement because the closing has not occurred by the end date and at the time of such termination the Company could have terminated the\n1073: merger agreement because of a Parent Breach Termination or a Parent Failure to Close Termination.\n1074: ​\n1075: Reimbursement of Expenses (Page 72)\n1076: If the merger agreement is terminated under specified circumstances, then the Company will be required to reimburse Parent for all reasonable out-of-pocket expenses incurred by Parent,\n1077: Merger Sub or their respective affiliates and all out-of-pocket fees and expenses of the financing sources for which Parent, Merger Sub, the Buyer Group or their respective affiliates may be\n1078: responsible for in connection with the merger agreement and the transactions contemplated by the merger agreement up to a maximum amount of $15 million. If the Company subsequently becomes\n1079: obligated to pay a termination fee to Parent, any such expense reimbursement paid by the Company will reduce the amount of any termination fee payable to Parent.\n1080: ~~~ PAGE 9 ~~~\n1081: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1082: Rights of Appraisal (Page 79 and Annex C)\n1083: Under Delaware law, holders of our common stock who do not vote in favor of the adoption of the merger agreement, who properly demand appraisal of their shares of common stock and who\n1084: otherwise comply with all the requirements of Section 262 of the General Corporation Law of the State of Delaware, referred to as the “DGCL,” will be entitled to seek appraisal for, and\n1085: obtain payment in cash for the judicially determined fair value of, their shares of common stock in lieu of receiving the merger consideration if the merger is completed. This value could be\n1086: more than, the same as, or less than the merger consideration. Any holder of common stock intending to exercise appraisal rights, among other things, must submit a written demand for\n1087: appraisal to us prior to the vote on the proposal to adopt the merger agreement and must not vote in favor of the proposal to adopt the merger agreement and must otherwise strictly comply\n1088: with all of the procedures required by Delaware law. The relevant provisions of the DGCL are included as Annex C to this proxy statement. You are encouraged to read these provisions\n1089: carefully and in their entirety. If you hold your shares of common stock through a bank, brokerage firm or other nominee and you wish to exercise appraisal rights, you should consult with\n1090: your bank, brokerage firm or other nominee to determine the appropriate procedures for the making of a demand for appraisal by such bank, brokerage firm or nominee. Moreover, due to the\n1091: complexity of the procedures for exercising the right to seek appraisal, stockholders who are considering exercising such rights are encouraged to seek the advice of legal counsel. Failure\n1092: to strictly comply with these provisions will result in loss of the right of appraisal.\n1093: ~~~ PAGE 10 ~~~\n1094: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1095: ​\n1096: QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS ABOUT THE SPECIAL MEETING AND THE MERGER\n1097: The following questions and answers address briefly some questions you may have regarding the special meeting, the merger agreement and the merger. These questions and answers may not\n1098: address all questions that may be important to you as a stockholder of the Company. Please refer to the more detailed information contained elsewhere in this proxy statement, the annexes to\n1099: this proxy statement and the documents referred to or incorporated by reference in this proxy statement.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1100]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/petsmart/paged.txt, lines 820-1099\n820: •\n821: the delivery of an officer’s certificate by the Company certifying that the conditions in the two bullets above have been satisfied.\n822: ​\n823: The obligation of the Company to complete the merger is subject to the satisfaction or waiver of the following additional conditions:\n824: •\n825: the accuracy of the representations and warranties of Parent and Merger Sub (subject to materiality, knowledge and other qualifications specified in the merger agreement);\n826: ​\n827: •\n828: Parent’s and Merger Sub’s performance of and compliance with their obligations and covenants under the merger agreement in all material respects; and\n829: ​\n830: •\n831: the delivery of an officer’s certificate by Parent certifying that the conditions in the two bullets above have been satisfied.\n832: ​\n833: When the Merger Becomes Effective (Page 53)\n834: We anticipate completing the merger in the first half of 2015, subject to the adoption of the merger agreement by the Company’s stockholders as specified herein and the satisfaction of the\n835: other closing conditions.\n836: Recommendation of the Company’s Board of Directors (Pages 17 and 30)\n837: The board unanimously (i) determined that the merger agreement and the merger are advisable and in the best interests of the Company and its stockholders, (ii) approved the execution,\n838: delivery and performance of the merger agreement, and (iii) resolved to recommend the adoption of the merger agreement by the stockholders of the Company and directed that such matter be\n839: submitted for consideration of the stockholders of the Company at the special meeting. The board unanimously recommends that the stockholders of the Company vote “\n840: FOR\n841: ” the merger proposal, “\n842: FOR\n843: ” the compensation proposal and “\n844: FOR\n845: ” the adjournment proposal. For a description of the reasons considered by the board in deciding to recommend approval of the merger proposal, see “\n846: The Merger (Proposal 1) — Reasons for the Merger.\n847: ”\n848: ~~~ PAGE 4 ~~~\n849: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n850: Opinion of J.P. Morgan (Page 30 and Annex B)\n851: At the meeting of the board on December 13, 2014, J.P. Morgan Securities LLC (“J.P. Morgan”) rendered its oral opinion, subsequently confirmed in writing on December 14, 2014, to the board\n852: that, as of such date and based upon and subject to the factors, assumptions and limitations set forth in its opinion, the merger consideration to be paid to the holders of the Company’s\n853: common stock in the merger was fair, from a financial point of view, to such holders.\n854: The full text of the written opinion of J.P. Morgan dated December 14, 2014, which sets forth the assumptions made, procedures followed, matters considered and limitations on the review\n855: undertaken in rendering its opinion, is attached as Annex B to this proxy statement and is incorporated herein by reference. The Company urges stockholders to read the opinion in its\n856: entirety. J.P. Morgan’s written opinion is addressed to the board, is directed only to the merger consideration paid to the holders of the Company’s common stock in the merger and does not\n857: constitute a recommendation to any stockholder of the Company as to how such stockholder should vote with respect to the merger or any other matter. The summary of the opinion of J.P. Morgan\n858: set forth in this proxy statement is qualified in its entirety by reference to the full text of such opinion.\n859: Treatment of Company Stock Options and Company RSU Awards (Page 45)\n860: Company Options\n861: .   Except as otherwise agreed in writing between any holder and Parent, each option to purchase a share of common stock (a “Company option”) whether vested or unvested, that is outstanding\n862: immediately prior to the effective time of the merger will, as of the effective time of the merger, become fully vested and be converted into the right to receive an amount in cash, less\n863: applicable tax withholdings, equal to the product obtained by multiplying (a) the excess, if any, of the merger consideration over the exercise price per share of the Company option, by (b)\n864: the total number of shares subject to the Company option.\n865: Company RSU Awards\n866: .   Except as otherwise agreed in writing between any holder and Parent, each award of restricted stock units that corresponds to shares of common stock (a “Company RSU award”) whether\n867: vested or unvested, that is outstanding immediately prior to the effective time of the merger, will, as of the effective time of the merger, become fully vested and be converted into the\n868: right to receive an amount in cash, less applicable tax withholdings, equal to (1) the product obtained by multiplying (a) the total number of shares subject to the Company RSU award, by (b)\n869: the merger consideration, plus (2) any dividends accrued with respect to the Company RSU award.\n870: Company PSU Awards\n871: .   Except as otherwise agreed in writing between any holder and Parent, each performance stock unit award that corresponds to shares (a “Company PSU award”) that is outstanding immediately\n872: prior to the effective time of the merger will become fully vested (based on actual performance, in the case of awards whose performance period ends prior to the effective time of the\n873: merger, at 109.6% of target levels, in the case of awards granted in the 2013 fiscal year, and at 150.0% of target levels, in the case of awards granted in the 2014 fiscal year) and be\n874: converted into the right to receive an amount in cash, less applicable tax withholdings, equal to (1) the product obtained by multiplying (a) the total number of shares subject to the\n875: Company PSU award by (b) the merger consideration, plus (2) any dividends accrued with respect to the Company PSU award.\n876: Company Restricted Stock\n877: .   Except as otherwise agreed in writing between any holder and Parent, effective as of immediately prior to the effective time of the merger, each then-outstanding restricted share of\n878: common stock (the “Company restricted stock”) will automatically become fully vested and the restrictions thereon will lapse, and each such share of Company restricted stock will be\n879: cancelled and converted into the right to receive an amount in cash, less applicable tax withholdings, equal to the merger consideration, plus any dividends accrued with respect to the\n880: Company restricted stock.\n881: Interests of the Company’s Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger (Page 45)\n882: In considering the recommendation of the board with respect to the merger proposal, you should be aware that some of the Company’s directors and executive officers have interests in the\n883: merger that are different from, or in addition to, the interests of the Company’s stockholders generally. Interests of officers and directors that may be different from or in addition to the\n884: interests of the Company’s stockholders include, among others:\n885: ~~~ PAGE 5 ~~~\n886: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n887: ​\n888: •\n889: The merger agreement provides for the accelerated vesting and cash-out of all outstanding Company equity awards.\n890: ​\n891: •\n892: The Company’s executive officers are participants in the Company’s Executive Change in Control and Severance Benefit Plan, which provides for enhanced severance benefits in the event of a\n893: qualifying termination of employment within three months prior to or 36 months following the completion of the merger.\n894: ​\n895: •\n896: Pursuant to the merger agreement, the Company has established a cash-based retention program to promote retention and to incentivize efforts to consummate the merger under which certain\n897: executive officers have been granted awards.\n898: ​\n899: •\n900: Under the Company’s 2005 Deferred Compensation Plan, all matching contributions will vest and participants will be entitled to receive full accelerated payment of their plan balances upon\n901: the occurrence of a change in control.\n902: ​\n903: •\n904: The Company’s directors and executive officers are entitled to continued indemnification and insurance coverage under indemnification agreements and the merger agreement.\n905: ​\n906: These interests are discussed in more detail in the section entitled “\n907: The Merger (Proposal 1) — Interests of the Company’s Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger\n908: ”. The board was aware of the different or additional interests set forth herein and considered such interests along with other matters in approving the merger agreement and the transactions\n909: contemplated thereby, including the merger.\n910: Financing (Page 40)\n911: Parent estimates that the total amount required to complete the merger and related transactions and pay related fees and expenses will be approximately $8.4 billion. Parent expects this\n912: amount to be funded through a combination of the following:\n913: •\n914: debt financing in an aggregate principal amount of up to approximately $6.2 billion as well as a $750 million senior secured asset-based revolving credit facility, a portion of which will be\n915: available at closing. Parent has received firm commitments from a consortium of financial institutions to provide the debt financing and revolving credit facility (see “\n916: The Merger (Proposal 1) — Financing — Debt Financing\n917: ”);\n918: ​\n919: •\n920: cash equity investments by members of the Buyer Group (other than Longview) in an aggregate amount up to approximately $1.83 billion and Longview’s contribution to Parent of  $250 million\n921: worth of the Company’s common stock immediately prior to the effective time of the merger. Parent has received equity commitments for the equity financing from the Buyer Group (other than\n922: Longview) and has entered into a rollover agreement with Longview with respect to its contribution of Company common stock to Parent (see “\n923: The Merger (Proposal 1) — Financing —  Equity Financing\n924: ”); and\n925: ​\n926: •\n927: approximately $425 million of cash is expected to be on hand in the Company and available at the closing.\n928: ​\n929: The consummation of the merger is not subject to a financing condition (although the funding of the debt and cash equity financing is subject to the satisfaction of the conditions set forth\n930: in the commitment letters under which the financing will be provided).\n931: Termination Fee Commitment Letters (Page 44)\n932: Each of the members of the Buyer Group (other than Longview) has entered into a termination fee commitment letter with Parent and the Company and has agreed on a several basis, and subject\n933: to other terms and conditions of the termination fee commitment letters, to pay to the Company (or if consented to by the Company, to purchase common equity securities of Parent to enable\n934: Parent to pay to the Company) an amount equal to its portion of the $510 million Parent termination fee and certain expense reimbursement that may be payable by Parent under the merger\n935: agreement, if and when due under the merger agreement. See “\n936: The Merger — Termination Fee Commitment Letters\n937: .”\n938: ~~~ PAGE 6 ~~~\n939: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n940: Material U.S. Federal Income Tax Consequences of the Merger (Page 49)\n941: The receipt of cash in exchange for shares of common stock pursuant to the merger will generally be a taxable transaction for U.S. federal income tax purposes. You should consult your own\n942: tax advisors regarding the particular tax consequences to you of the exchange of shares of common stock for cash pursuant to the merger in light of your particular circumstances (including\n943: the application and effect of any state, local or foreign income and other tax laws).\n944: Regulatory Approvals (Page 52)\n945: Under the HSR Act and related rules, certain transactions, including the merger, may not be completed until notifications have been given and information furnished to the Antitrust Division\n946: of the United States Department of Justice (“Antitrust Division”) and the Federal Trade Commission (“FTC”) and all statutory waiting period requirements have been satisfied or early\n947: termination has been granted by the applicable agencies. On December 24, 2014, both the Company and Parent filed their respective Notification and Report Forms with the Antitrust Division\n948: and the FTC. The 30-day waiting period under the HSR Act will expire at 11:59 pm on January 23, 2015, unless it is terminated early or otherwise extended. Both the Company and Parent\n949: received early termination of the HSR waiting period on January 7, 2015.\n950: The merger is also conditioned on the applicable waiting period under the Competition Act having expired or been terminated by the applicable government agencies. Under the Competition Act,\n951: the parties must file a pre-merger notification and observe the specified waiting period requirements before consummating the merger, unless the parties are exempted from such requirements\n952: through the issuance of an Advance Ruling Certificate (an “ARC”), or a “no-action” letter together with a waiver of the notification and waiting period requirements. On December 30, 2014,\n953: the parties submitted a request to the Commissioner of Competition in Canada for an ARC or, in the alternative, a “no-action” letter. The parties were issued an ARC pursuant to section 102\n954: of the Competition Act on January 14, 2015.\n955: No Solicitation (Page 61)\n956: Under the merger agreement, the Company and its affiliates and their respective representatives may not:\n957: •\n958: solicit, initiate or knowingly encourage or facilitate the making or submission of an alternative proposal (defined below under\n959: The Merger Agreement — Other Covenants and Agreements — No Solicitation\n960: ) or any inquiries, discussions or offers that constitute or could reasonably expected to lead to any alternative proposal;\n961: ​\n962: •\n963: participate in any discussions or negotiations regarding an alternative proposal with, or furnish any nonpublic information regarding the Company or its subsidiaries in connection with an\n964: alternative proposal to, any person that has made, or is considering making an alternative proposal (except to notify such person as to the non-solicitation provisions of the merger\n965: agreement); or\n966: ​\n967: •\n968: enter into any letter of intent, agreement in principle, merger or acquisition agreement or any other agreement relating to or providing for any alternative proposal (except for\n969: confidentiality agreements permitted under the merger agreement).\n970: ​\n971: But if, prior to obtaining the required vote of the Company’s stockholders to adopt the merger agreement, the Company receives an unsolicited alternative proposal from a third party that in\n972: the good faith judgment of the board, after consultation with outside legal and financial advisors, constitutes, or could reasonably be expected to result in, a “superior proposal” (defined\n973: below under\n974: The Merger Agreement — Other Covenants and Agreements — No Solicitation\n975: ), then the Company may take the following actions:\n976: •\n977: provide nonpublic information to the third party after entering into a confidentiality agreement acceptable under the terms of the merger agreement; and\n978: ​\n979: •\n980: engage in discussions or negotiations with a third party with respect to the alternative proposal.\n981: ​\n982: ​\n983: ~~~ PAGE 7 ~~~\n984: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n985: In order for the board to change its recommendation in response to a superior proposal or for the Company to terminate the merger agreement to accept a superior proposal, the Company must\n986: (among other obligations):\n987: •\n988: provide Parent written notice of the board’s intention to make a change in recommendation or to terminate the merger agreement with a description of the terms of the superior proposal;\n989: ​\n990: •\n991: provide to Parent in writing its reasons for making the change in recommendation or for terminating the merger agreement; and\n992: ​\n993: •\n994: provide Parent with three business days to propose changes to the terms of the merger agreement (which will be extended for additional two business day periods for any material change in the\n995: superior proposal), and during this period negotiate with Parent in good faith to make adjustments to the terms and conditions of the merger agreement.\n996: ​\n997: Following the end of such three business day period (and any extension thereto), after taking into account any revisions to the terms and conditions of the merger agreement proposed by\n998: Parent, the board must determine that the superior proposal remains superior to the merger proposal. If the Company terminates the merger agreement to enter into an agreement with a third\n999: party regarding a superior proposal or the board changes its recommendation in response to a superior proposal and the agreement is terminated by Parent, then the Company must pay a\n1000: termination fee of  $255 million to Parent (as described in more detail under\n1001: The Merger Agreement — Termination\n1002: ).\n1003: Termination (Page 70)\n1004: The Company and Parent may terminate the merger agreement by mutual written consent at any time before the effective time of the merger. In addition, either the Company or Parent may\n1005: terminate the merger agreement if:\n1006: •\n1007: the effective time of the merger has not occurred on or before June 14, 2015 (the “end date”), and the party seeking to terminate the merger agreement pursuant to this provision has not\n1008: breached in any material respect its obligations under the merger agreement in any manner that has contributed to the failure to consummate the merger on or before such date and has used the\n1009: efforts required of it to consummate the merger (including obtaining antitrust approvals) and to obtain the proceeds of the financings;\n1010: ​\n1011: •\n1012: a governmental entity of competent jurisdiction has issued or entered an injunction or similar order permanently enjoining or otherwise prohibiting the consummation of the merger and such\n1013: injunction has become final and non-appealable, so long as the party seeking to terminate the merger agreement pursuant to this provision has not breached in any material respect its\n1014: obligations under the merger agreement in any manner that has contributed to such injunction or order and has used the efforts required of it under the merger agreement to prevent, oppose\n1015: and remove such injunction or order; or\n1016: ​\n1017: •\n1018: the Company’s stockholders fail to adopt the merger agreement after the conclusion of a stockholder meeting (including any adjournments or postponements thereof) held for that purpose.\n1019: ​\n1020: The Company may terminate the merger agreement:\n1021: •\n1022: if Parent or Merger Sub has breached or failed to perform in any material respect any of its representations, warranties, covenants or other agreements contained in the merger agreement\n1023: which would result in the failure to satisfy a closing condition or would result in the failure of the closing to occur, subject to a 30 business day cure period if the breach or failure is\n1024: capable of being cured, so long as the Company is not then in material breach of any representation, warranty, agreement or covenant contained in the merger agreement (a “Parent Breach\n1025: Termination”);\n1026: ​\n1027: •\n1028: at any time prior to the Company stockholder meeting to accept a “superior proposal” (defined below under\n1029: The Merger Agreement — Other Covenants and Agreements — No Solicitation\n1030: ) so\n1031: ​\n1032: ​\n1033: ~~~ PAGE 8 ~~~\n1034: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1035: long as (i) prior to or substantially concurrent with such termination, the Company has paid a termination fee of  $255 million to Parent and (ii) promptly after such termination, the\n1036: Company enters into a definitive agreement with respect to such superior proposal; or\n1037: •\n1038: if  (i) the merger is not consummated by the second business day after the first date upon which Parent was required to consummate the closing and (ii) the Company was ready, willing, and\n1039: able to consummate the merger and provided written notice to Parent confirming such fact (a “Parent Failure to Close Termination”).\n1040: ​\n1041: Parent may terminate the merger agreement:\n1042: •\n1043: if the board takes an action that constitutes a change of recommendation (as defined below under\n1044: The Merger Agreement — Other Covenants and Agreements — No Solicitation\n1045: ) prior to the stockholder meeting to adopt the merger agreement; or\n1046: ​\n1047: •\n1048: if the Company breached or failed to perform in any material respect any of its representations, warranties, covenants or other agreements contained in the merger agreement which would\n1049: result in the failure to satisfy a closing condition or would result in the failure of the closing to occur, subject to a 30 business day cure period if the breach or failure is capable of\n1050: being cured, so long as Parent or Merger Sub is not then in material breach of any representation, warranty, agreement or covenant contained in the merger agreement.\n1051: ​\n1052: Termination Fees (Page 71)\n1053: The Company will pay to Parent a termination fee of  $255 million in the event that:\n1054: •\n1055: the Company has terminated the merger agreement before stockholder approval of the merger proposal is obtained in response to a superior proposal and promptly after such termination the\n1056: Company enters into a definitive agreement with respect to such superior proposal;\n1057: ​\n1058: •\n1059: Parent has terminated the merger agreement in the event of a change of recommendation; or\n1060: ​\n1061: •\n1062: the merger is terminated under specified conditions and concurrently with or within 12 months after such termination, the Company enters into a definitive agreement providing for a\n1063: qualifying transaction (as defined below under “\n1064: The Merger Agreement — Termination Fees\n1065: ”) or completes a qualifying transaction.\n1066: ​\n1067: Parent will pay to the Company a reverse termination fee of  $510 million in cash in the event that:\n1068: •\n1069: the Company has terminated the merger agreement as a result of a Parent Breach Termination or a Parent Failure to Close Termination; or\n1070: ​\n1071: •\n1072: the Company or Parent has terminated the merger agreement because the closing has not occurred by the end date and at the time of such termination the Company could have terminated the\n1073: merger agreement because of a Parent Breach Termination or a Parent Failure to Close Termination.\n1074: ​\n1075: Reimbursement of Expenses (Page 72)\n1076: If the merger agreement is terminated under specified circumstances, then the Company will be required to reimburse Parent for all reasonable out-of-pocket expenses incurred by Parent,\n1077: Merger Sub or their respective affiliates and all out-of-pocket fees and expenses of the financing sources for which Parent, Merger Sub, the Buyer Group or their respective affiliates may be\n1078: responsible for in connection with the merger agreement and the transactions contemplated by the merger agreement up to a maximum amount of $15 million. If the Company subsequently becomes\n1079: obligated to pay a termination fee to Parent, any such expense reimbursement paid by the Company will reduce the amount of any termination fee payable to Parent.\n1080: ~~~ PAGE 9 ~~~\n1081: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1082: Rights of Appraisal (Page 79 and Annex C)\n1083: Under Delaware law, holders of our common stock who do not vote in favor of the adoption of the merger agreement, who properly demand appraisal of their shares of common stock and who\n1084: otherwise comply with all the requirements of Section 262 of the General Corporation Law of the State of Delaware, referred to as the “DGCL,” will be entitled to seek appraisal for, and\n1085: obtain payment in cash for the judicially determined fair value of, their shares of common stock in lieu of receiving the merger consideration if the merger is completed. This value could be\n1086: more than, the same as, or less than the merger consideration. Any holder of common stock intending to exercise appraisal rights, among other things, must submit a written demand for\n1087: appraisal to us prior to the vote on the proposal to adopt the merger agreement and must not vote in favor of the proposal to adopt the merger agreement and must otherwise strictly comply\n1088: with all of the procedures required by Delaware law. The relevant provisions of the DGCL are included as Annex C to this proxy statement. You are encouraged to read these provisions\n1089: carefully and in their entirety. If you hold your shares of common stock through a bank, brokerage firm or other nominee and you wish to exercise appraisal rights, you should consult with\n1090: your bank, brokerage firm or other nominee to determine the appropriate procedures for the making of a demand for appraisal by such bank, brokerage firm or nominee. Moreover, due to the\n1091: complexity of the procedures for exercising the right to seek appraisal, stockholders who are considering exercising such rights are encouraged to seek the advice of legal counsel. Failure\n1092: to strictly comply with these provisions will result in loss of the right of appraisal.\n1093: ~~~ PAGE 10 ~~~\n1094: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1095: ​\n1096: QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS ABOUT THE SPECIAL MEETING AND THE MERGER\n1097: The following questions and answers address briefly some questions you may have regarding the special meeting, the merger agreement and the merger. These questions and answers may not\n1098: address all questions that may be important to you as a stockholder of the Company. Please refer to the more detailed information contained elsewhere in this proxy statement, the annexes to\n1099: this proxy statement and the documents referred to or incorporated by reference in this proxy statement.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1100]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"/tmp/opencode/petsmart/paged.txt\", \"offset\": 820, \"limit\": 280}"},"time":{"start":1789824958132,"end":1789824958302}}}}
