{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824966258,"sessionID":"ses_f461f6897ffeZJz46cQwZkb8iB","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9e19db50011Axo6GJg2UdUk1","sessionID":"ses_f461f6897ffeZJz46cQwZkb8iB","messageID":"msg_0b9e196e7001Rbr404zhuz4yvB","type":"tool","id":"call_00_R0r56vUxQHqLOr0xtd1p5548","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/tmp/opencode/petsmart/paged.txt","offset":1100,"limit":330},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/petsmart/paged.txt, lines 1100-1429\n1100: Q:\n1101: Why am I receiving this proxy statement?\n1102: ​\n1103: A:\n1104: On December 14, 2014, the Company entered into the merger agreement providing for the merger of Merger Sub, a wholly owned subsidiary of Parent, with and into the Company, with the Company\n1105: surviving the merger as a wholly owned subsidiary of Parent. You are receiving this proxy statement in connection with the solicitation of proxies by the board in favor of the proposal to\n1106: adopt the merger agreement and the other matters to be voted on at the special meeting.\n1107: ​\n1108: Q:\n1109: What is the proposed transaction?\n1110: ​\n1111: A:\n1112: The proposed transaction is the merger of Merger Sub with and into the Company pursuant to the merger agreement. Following the effective time of the merger, the Company will be privately\n1113: held as a wholly owned subsidiary of Parent.\n1114: ​\n1115: Q:\n1116: What will I receive in the merger?\n1117: ​\n1118: A:\n1119: If the merger is completed, you will be entitled to receive $83.00 in cash, without interest and less any applicable withholding taxes, for each share of our common stock that you own. For\n1120: example, if you own 100 shares of common stock, you will be entitled to receive $8,300 in cash in exchange for your shares of common stock, less any applicable withholding taxes. You will\n1121: not be entitled to receive shares in the surviving corporation or in Parent.\n1122: ​\n1123: Q:\n1124: Where and when is the special meeting?\n1125: ​\n1126: A:\n1127: The special meeting will take place at the Sheraton Crescent Hotel, 2620 W. Dunlap Avenue, Phoenix, AZ 85021 on March 6, 2015, at 1:00 p.m., local time.\n1128: ​\n1129: Q:\n1130: What matters will be voted on at the special meeting?\n1131: ​\n1132: A:\n1133: You will be asked to consider and vote on the following proposals:\n1134: ​\n1135: •\n1136: to adopt the merger agreement;\n1137: ​\n1138: •\n1139: to approve, on an advisory (non-binding) basis, specified compensation that may be paid or become payable to the named executive officers of the Company in connection with the merger; and\n1140: ​\n1141: •\n1142: to approve the adjournment of the special meeting from time to time to solicit additional proxies, if necessary or appropriate, if there are insufficient votes at the time of the special\n1143: meeting to approve the proposal to adopt the merger agreement.\n1144: ​\n1145: Q:\n1146: What vote of our stockholders is required to approve the merger agreement?\n1147: ​\n1148: A:\n1149: Under Delaware law, stockholders holding at least a majority of the shares of common stock outstanding at the close of business on the record date for the determination of stockholders\n1150: entitled to vote at the meeting must vote\n1151: “FOR”\n1152: the merger proposal. In addition, under the merger agreement, the receipt of such required vote is a condition to the consummation of the merger. A failure to vote your shares of common\n1153: stock or an abstention from voting will have the same effect as a vote against the merger proposal.\n1154: ​\n1155: As of the close of business on January 29, 2015, the record date for the special meeting, there were 99,455,151 shares of common stock outstanding.\n1156: ~~~ PAGE 11 ~~~\n1157: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1158: ​\n1159: Q:\n1160: How will our directors and executive officers vote on the proposal to adopt the merger agreement?\n1161: ​\n1162: A:\n1163: The directors and executive officers of the Company have informed the Company that as of the date of this proxy statement, they intend to vote in favor of the merger proposal.\n1164: ​\n1165: As of the close of business on January 29, 2015, the record date for the special meeting, the directors and current executive officers owned, in the aggregate, 0.4% of the outstanding common\n1166: stock of the Company entitled to vote at the special meeting.\n1167: Q:\n1168: Do any of the Company’s directors or executive officers have interests in the merger that may differ from or be in addition to my interests as a stockholder?\n1169: ​\n1170: A:\n1171: Yes. In considering the recommendation of the board with respect to the merger proposal, you should be aware that our directors and executive officers have interests in the merger that are\n1172: different from, or in addition to, the interests of our stockholders generally. The board was aware of and considered these differing interests, among other matters, in evaluating and\n1173: negotiating the merger agreement and the merger, and in unanimously recommending that the merger agreement be adopted by the Company stockholders. See “\n1174: The Merger (Proposal 1) — Interests of the Company’s Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger.\n1175: ”\n1176: ​\n1177: Q:\n1178: What vote of our stockholders is required to approve other matters to be presented at the special meeting?\n1179: ​\n1180: A:\n1181: The compensation proposal and the adjournment proposal each require the affirmative vote of the holders of a majority of the shares of common stock present in person or represented by proxy\n1182: at the special meeting and entitled to vote thereon.\n1183: ​\n1184: Q:\n1185: Are there any voting agreements with existing stockholders?\n1186: ​\n1187: A:\n1188: In connection with the merger agreement, the Company, Parent and Longview entered into the voting agreement, pursuant to which Longview has agreed, among other things and subject to certain\n1189: conditions, to vote 7,424,591 shares of common stock in favor of the merger proposal and against any proposal made in opposition to or in competition with the merger or the merger agreement\n1190: that is not approved by the board.\n1191: ​\n1192: As of the close of business on the record date, 7,424,591 shares of common stock represented approximately 7.5% of the total outstanding common stock on such date.\n1193: Q:\n1194: What is a quorum?\n1195: ​\n1196: A:\n1197: A quorum will be present if holders of a majority of the shares of common stock outstanding as of the close of business on the record date are present in person or represented by proxy at\n1198: the special meeting. If a quorum is not present at the special meeting, the special meeting may be adjourned or postponed from time to time until a quorum is obtained.\n1199: ​\n1200: If you submit a proxy but fail to provide voting instructions or abstain on any of the proposals listed on the proxy card, your shares will be counted for purpose of determining whether a\n1201: quorum is present at the special meeting.\n1202: If your shares are held in “street name” by your broker, bank or other nominee and you do not tell the nominee how to vote your shares, these shares will not be counted for purposes of\n1203: determining whether a quorum is present for the transaction of business at the special meeting.\n1204: Q:\n1205: How does the board recommend that I vote?\n1206: ​\n1207: A:\n1208: The board unanimously recommends that our stockholders vote\n1209: “FOR”\n1210:  the merger proposal. The board also unanimously recommends that our stockholders vote\n1211: “FOR”\n1212:  the compensation proposal and\n1213: “FOR”\n1214:  the adjournment proposal.\n1215: ​\n1216: Q:\n1217: What effects will the merger have on PetSmart?\n1218: ​\n1219: A:\n1220: Our common stock is currently registered under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, as amended, referred to as the “Exchange Act,” and is quoted on the NASDAQ Global Select Market, referred\n1221: to as\n1222: ​\n1223: ​\n1224: ~~~ PAGE 12 ~~~\n1225: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1226: the “NASDAQ,” under the symbol “PETM.” As a result of the merger, the Company will cease to be a publicly traded company and will be wholly owned by Parent. Following the consummation of the\n1227: merger, the registration of our common stock and our reporting obligations under the Exchange Act will be terminated. In addition, upon the consummation of the merger, our common stock will\n1228: no longer be listed on any stock exchange or quotation system, including the NASDAQ.\n1229: Q:\n1230: What happens if the merger is not consummated?\n1231: ​\n1232: A:\n1233: If the merger agreement is not approved by the Company’s stockholders, or if the merger is not consummated for any other reason, the Company’s stockholders will not receive any payment for\n1234: their shares in connection with the merger. Instead, the Company will remain a public company and shares of our common stock will continue to be listed and traded on the NASDAQ. Under\n1235: specified circumstances, the Company may be required to pay Parent a termination fee of  $255 million and/or reimburse Parent’s and the Buyer Group expenses up to a maximum amount of  $15\n1236: million (with any expense reimbursement payable to Parent and the Buyer Group reducing the amount of any termination fee payable by the Company), or Parent may be required to pay the Company\n1237: a termination fee of  $510 million or reimburse the Company for certain expenses or losses incurred in connection with the transactions contemplated by the merger agreement. See “\n1238: The Merger Agreement — Termination Fees\n1239: ” and “\n1240: The Merger Agreement — Reimbursement of Expenses.\n1241: ”\n1242: ​\n1243: Q:\n1244: What will happen if stockholders do not approve the advisory proposal on executive compensation payable to the Company’s named executive officers in connection with the merger?\n1245: ​\n1246: A:\n1247: The approval of this proposal is not a condition to the completion of the merger. The SEC rules require the Company to seek approval on a non-binding, advisory basis of certain payments that\n1248: will or may be made to the Company’s named executive officers in connection with the merger. The vote on this proposal is an advisory vote and will not be binding on the Company or Parent.\n1249: If the merger agreement is adopted by the stockholders and the merger is completed, the merger-related compensation may be paid to the Company’s named executive officers even if stockholders\n1250: fail to approve this proposal.\n1251: ​\n1252: Q:\n1253: What do I need to do now? How do I vote my shares of common stock?\n1254: ​\n1255: A:\n1256: We urge you to read this proxy statement carefully, including its annexes and the documents referred to as incorporated by reference in this proxy statement, and to consider how the merger\n1257: affects you.\n1258: Your vote is important.\n1259:  If you are a stockholder of record, you can ensure that your shares are voted at the special meeting by submitting your proxy via:\n1260: ​\n1261: •\n1262: mail, using the enclosed postage-paid envelope;\n1263: ​\n1264: •\n1265: telephone, using the toll-free number listed on each proxy card; or\n1266: ​\n1267: •\n1268: the Internet, at the address provided on each proxy card.\n1269: ​\n1270: If you hold your shares in “street name” through a broker, bank or other nominee you should follow the directions provided by your broker, bank or other nominee regarding how to instruct\n1271: your broker, bank or other nominee to vote your shares. Without those instructions, your shares will not be voted, which will have the same effect as voting “\n1272: AGAINST\n1273: ” the merger proposal.\n1274: Q:\n1275: Can I revoke my proxy?\n1276: ​\n1277: Yes. You can revoke your proxy at any time before the vote is taken at the special meeting. If you are a stockholder of record, you may revoke your proxy by notifying the Company’s Corporate\n1278: Secretary in writing at PetSmart, Inc., Attn: Corporate Secretary, 19601 North 27th Avenue, Phoenix, Arizona 85027, or by submitting a new proxy by telephone, the Internet or mail, in each\n1279: case, dated after the date of the proxy being revoked. In addition, you may revoke your proxy by attending the special meeting and voting in person (simply attending the special meeting will\n1280: not cause your proxy to be revoked). Please note that if you hold your shares in “street name” and you have instructed a broker,\n1281: ~~~ PAGE 13 ~~~\n1282: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1283: bank or other nominee to vote your shares, the above-described options for revoking your voting instructions do not apply, and instead you must follow the instructions received from your\n1284: broker, bank or other nominee to revoke your voting instructions.\n1285: Q:\n1286: What happens if I do not vote?\n1287: ​\n1288: A:\n1289: The vote to adopt the merger agreement is based on the total number of shares of common stock outstanding as of the close of business on the record date, not just the shares that are voted.\n1290: If you do not vote, it will have the same effect as a vote “\n1291: AGAINST\n1292: ” the proposal to adopt the merger agreement.\n1293: ​\n1294: Q:\n1295: Will my shares held in “street name” or another form of record ownership be combined for voting purposes with shares I hold of record?\n1296: ​\n1297: A:\n1298: No. Because any shares you may hold in “street name” will be deemed to be held by a different stockholder than any shares you hold of record, any shares so held will not be combined for\n1299: voting purposes with shares you hold of record. Similarly, if you own shares in various registered forms, such as jointly with your spouse, as trustee of a trust or as custodian for a minor,\n1300: you will receive, and will need to sign and return, a separate proxy card for those shares because they are held in a different form of record ownership. Shares held by a corporation or\n1301: business entity must be voted by an authorized officer of the entity. Shares held in an individual retirement account must be voted under the rules governing the account.\n1302: ​\n1303: Q:\n1304: What happens if I sell my shares of common stock before completion of the merger?\n1305: ​\n1306: A:\n1307: If you transfer your shares of common stock, you will have transferred your right to receive the merger consideration in the merger. In order to receive the merger consideration, you must\n1308: hold your shares of common stock through completion of the merger.\n1309: ​\n1310: The record date for stockholders entitled to vote at the special meeting is earlier than the date on which the merger will be consummated. So, if you transfer your shares of common stock\n1311: after the record date but before the special meeting, you will have transferred your right to receive the merger consideration in the merger, but retained the right to vote at the special\n1312: meeting.\n1313: Q:\n1314: Should I send in my stock certificates or other evidence of ownership now?\n1315: ​\n1316: A:\n1317: No, do not send in your certificates now\n1318: . After the merger is completed, if you hold shares represented by certificates, you will receive a letter of transmittal from the paying agent for the merger with detailed written\n1319: instructions for exchanging your shares of common stock for the merger consideration and if you hold book entry shares you will receive a check or wire transfer for the merger consideration\n1320: with respect to such shares. If your shares of common stock are held in “street name” by your broker, bank or other nominee, you may receive instructions from your broker, bank or other\n1321: nominee as to what action, if any, you need to take to effect the surrender of your “street name” shares in exchange for the merger consideration.\n1322: ​\n1323: Q:\n1324: I do not know where my stock certificate is — how will I get the merger consideration for my shares?\n1325: ​\n1326: A:\n1327: If the merger is completed, the transmittal materials you will receive after the completion of the merger will include the procedures that you must follow if you cannot locate your stock\n1328: certificate. This will include an affidavit that you will need to sign attesting to the loss of your stock certificate. You may also be required to provide a customary indemnity agreement in\n1329: order to cover any potential loss.\n1330: ​\n1331: Q:\n1332: Am I entitled to exercise appraisal rights instead of receiving the merger consideration for my shares of common stock?\n1333: ​\n1334: A:\n1335: If you comply with all the requirements of Section 262 of the DGCL (including not voting in favor of the adoption of the merger agreement), you are entitled to have the “fair value” (as\n1336: defined pursuant to Section 262 of the DGCL) of your shares of common stock determined by the Court of Chancery of the State of Delaware and to receive payment based on that valuation\n1337: instead of receiving the merger\n1338: ​\n1339: ​\n1340: ~~~ PAGE 14 ~~~\n1341: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1342: consideration. The ultimate amount you would receive in an appraisal proceeding may be more than, the same as or less than the amount you would have received under the merger agreement. To\n1343: exercise your appraisal rights, you must comply with the requirements of the DGCL. See “\n1344: Rights of Appraisal\n1345: ” and the text of the Delaware appraisal rights statute, Section 262 of the DGCL, which is reproduced in its entirety as Annex C to this proxy statement.\n1346: Q:\n1347: Will I have to pay taxes on the merger consideration I receive?\n1348: ​\n1349: A:\n1350: The receipt of cash in exchange for shares of common stock pursuant to the merger will generally be a taxable transaction for U.S. federal income tax purposes. You should consult your own\n1351: tax advisors regarding the particular tax consequences to you of the exchange of shares of common stock for cash pursuant to the merger in light of your particular circumstances (including\n1352: the application and effect of any state, local or foreign income and other tax laws).\n1353: ​\n1354: Q:\n1355: What does it mean if I get more than one proxy card or voting instruction card?\n1356: ​\n1357: A:\n1358: If your shares are registered differently or are held in more than one account, you will receive more than one proxy card or voting instruction card. Please complete and return all of the\n1359: proxy cards or voting instruction cards you receive (or submit each of your proxies by telephone or the Internet, if available to you) to ensure that all of your shares are voted.\n1360: ​\n1361: Q:\n1362: What is householding and how does it affect me?\n1363: ​\n1364: A:\n1365: The SEC permits companies to send a single set of proxy materials to any household at which two or more stockholders reside, unless contrary instructions have been received, but only if the\n1366: applicable company provides advance notice and follows certain procedures. In such cases, each stockholder continues to receive a separate notice of the meeting and proxy card. Certain\n1367: brokerage firms may have instituted householding for beneficial owners of common stock held through brokerage firms. If your family has multiple accounts holding common stock, you may have\n1368: already received householding notification from your broker. Please contact your broker directly if you have any questions or require additional copies of this proxy statement. The broker\n1369: will arrange for delivery of a separate copy of this proxy statement promptly upon your written or oral request. You may decide at any time to revoke your decision to household, and thereby\n1370: receive multiple copies.\n1371: ​\n1372: Q:\n1373: Who can help answer my other questions?\n1374: ​\n1375: A:\n1376: If you have more questions about the merger, or require assistance in submitting your proxy or voting your shares or need additional copies of the proxy statement or the enclosed proxy card,\n1377: please contact Innisfree M&A Incorporated, which is acting as the proxy solicitation agent for the Company in connection with the merger.\n1378: ​\n1379: Innisfree M&A Incorporated\n1380: 501 Madison Avenue, 20th Floor, New York, NY 10022\n1381: Stockholders may call toll-free: (877) 456-3510\n1382: Banks & Brokers may call collect: (212) 750-5833\n1383: ~~~ PAGE 15 ~~~\n1384: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1385: ​\n1386: CAUTIONARY STATEMENT CONCERNING FORWARD-LOOKING STATEMENTS\n1387: This proxy statement, and the documents incorporated by reference in this proxy statement, include “forward-looking statements” within the meaning of the Private Securities Litigation Reform\n1388: Act of 1995, which are identified by the use of the words “believe,” “expect,” “may,” “could,” “should,” “plan,” “project,” “anticipate,” “intend,” “estimate,” “will,” “contemplate,” “would”\n1389: and similar expressions that contemplate future events. Such forward-looking statements are based on management’s reasonable current assumptions and expectations, including the expected\n1390: completion and timing of the merger and other information relating to the merger. You should be aware that forward-looking statements involve a number of assumptions, risks and uncertainties\n1391: that could cause the actual results to differ materially from such forward-looking statements. Although we believe that the expectations reflected in these forward-looking statements are\n1392: reasonable, we cannot assure you that the actual results or developments we anticipate will be realized, or even if realized, that they will have the expected effects on the business or\n1393: operations of the Company. These forward-looking statements speak only as of the date on which the statements were made and we undertake no obligation to update or revise any forward-looking\n1394: statements made in this proxy statement or elsewhere as a result of new information, future events or otherwise, except as required by law. In addition to other factors and matters contained\n1395: in or incorporated by reference in this document, we believe the following factors could cause actual results to differ materially from those discussed in the forward-looking statements:\n1396: •\n1397: the occurrence of any event, change or other circumstance that could give rise to the termination of the merger agreement;\n1398: ​\n1399: •\n1400: the failure to obtain the required vote of the Company’s stockholders to adopt the merger agreement, the failure to obtain any required regulatory approval, or the failure to satisfy any of\n1401: the other closing conditions to the merger, and any delay in connection with the foregoing;\n1402: ​\n1403: •\n1404: the failure to obtain the necessary financing;\n1405: ​\n1406: •\n1407: risks related to disruption of management’s attention from the Company’s ongoing business operations due to the pendency of the merger;\n1408: ​\n1409: •\n1410: the effect of the announcement of the merger on the ability of the Company to retain and hire key personnel and maintain relationships with its customers, suppliers, and others with whom it\n1411: does business, or on its operating results and business generally;\n1412: ​\n1413: •\n1414: changes in general economic conditions;\n1415: ​\n1416: •\n1417: the outcome of any legal proceedings that have been or may be instituted against the Company and others relating to the merger agreement; and\n1418: ​\n1419: •\n1420: other risks detailed in our filings with the SEC, including our most recent filings on Forms 10-K and 10-Qs. See “\n1421: Where You Can Find Additional Information.\n1422: ”\n1423: ​\n1424: Many of the factors that will determine our future results are beyond our ability to control or predict. In light of the significant uncertainties inherent in the forward-looking statements\n1425: contained herein, readers of this proxy statement should not place undue reliance on forward-looking statements, which reflect management’s views only as of the date hereof. We cannot\n1426: guarantee any future results, levels of activity, performance or achievements.\n1427: ~~~ PAGE 16 ~~~\n1428: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1429: ​\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1430]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/petsmart/paged.txt, lines 1100-1429\n1100: Q:\n1101: Why am I receiving this proxy statement?\n1102: ​\n1103: A:\n1104: On December 14, 2014, the Company entered into the merger agreement providing for the merger of Merger Sub, a wholly owned subsidiary of Parent, with and into the Company, with the Company\n1105: surviving the merger as a wholly owned subsidiary of Parent. You are receiving this proxy statement in connection with the solicitation of proxies by the board in favor of the proposal to\n1106: adopt the merger agreement and the other matters to be voted on at the special meeting.\n1107: ​\n1108: Q:\n1109: What is the proposed transaction?\n1110: ​\n1111: A:\n1112: The proposed transaction is the merger of Merger Sub with and into the Company pursuant to the merger agreement. Following the effective time of the merger, the Company will be privately\n1113: held as a wholly owned subsidiary of Parent.\n1114: ​\n1115: Q:\n1116: What will I receive in the merger?\n1117: ​\n1118: A:\n1119: If the merger is completed, you will be entitled to receive $83.00 in cash, without interest and less any applicable withholding taxes, for each share of our common stock that you own. For\n1120: example, if you own 100 shares of common stock, you will be entitled to receive $8,300 in cash in exchange for your shares of common stock, less any applicable withholding taxes. You will\n1121: not be entitled to receive shares in the surviving corporation or in Parent.\n1122: ​\n1123: Q:\n1124: Where and when is the special meeting?\n1125: ​\n1126: A:\n1127: The special meeting will take place at the Sheraton Crescent Hotel, 2620 W. Dunlap Avenue, Phoenix, AZ 85021 on March 6, 2015, at 1:00 p.m., local time.\n1128: ​\n1129: Q:\n1130: What matters will be voted on at the special meeting?\n1131: ​\n1132: A:\n1133: You will be asked to consider and vote on the following proposals:\n1134: ​\n1135: •\n1136: to adopt the merger agreement;\n1137: ​\n1138: •\n1139: to approve, on an advisory (non-binding) basis, specified compensation that may be paid or become payable to the named executive officers of the Company in connection with the merger; and\n1140: ​\n1141: •\n1142: to approve the adjournment of the special meeting from time to time to solicit additional proxies, if necessary or appropriate, if there are insufficient votes at the time of the special\n1143: meeting to approve the proposal to adopt the merger agreement.\n1144: ​\n1145: Q:\n1146: What vote of our stockholders is required to approve the merger agreement?\n1147: ​\n1148: A:\n1149: Under Delaware law, stockholders holding at least a majority of the shares of common stock outstanding at the close of business on the record date for the determination of stockholders\n1150: entitled to vote at the meeting must vote\n1151: “FOR”\n1152: the merger proposal. In addition, under the merger agreement, the receipt of such required vote is a condition to the consummation of the merger. A failure to vote your shares of common\n1153: stock or an abstention from voting will have the same effect as a vote against the merger proposal.\n1154: ​\n1155: As of the close of business on January 29, 2015, the record date for the special meeting, there were 99,455,151 shares of common stock outstanding.\n1156: ~~~ PAGE 11 ~~~\n1157: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1158: ​\n1159: Q:\n1160: How will our directors and executive officers vote on the proposal to adopt the merger agreement?\n1161: ​\n1162: A:\n1163: The directors and executive officers of the Company have informed the Company that as of the date of this proxy statement, they intend to vote in favor of the merger proposal.\n1164: ​\n1165: As of the close of business on January 29, 2015, the record date for the special meeting, the directors and current executive officers owned, in the aggregate, 0.4% of the outstanding common\n1166: stock of the Company entitled to vote at the special meeting.\n1167: Q:\n1168: Do any of the Company’s directors or executive officers have interests in the merger that may differ from or be in addition to my interests as a stockholder?\n1169: ​\n1170: A:\n1171: Yes. In considering the recommendation of the board with respect to the merger proposal, you should be aware that our directors and executive officers have interests in the merger that are\n1172: different from, or in addition to, the interests of our stockholders generally. The board was aware of and considered these differing interests, among other matters, in evaluating and\n1173: negotiating the merger agreement and the merger, and in unanimously recommending that the merger agreement be adopted by the Company stockholders. See “\n1174: The Merger (Proposal 1) — Interests of the Company’s Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger.\n1175: ”\n1176: ​\n1177: Q:\n1178: What vote of our stockholders is required to approve other matters to be presented at the special meeting?\n1179: ​\n1180: A:\n1181: The compensation proposal and the adjournment proposal each require the affirmative vote of the holders of a majority of the shares of common stock present in person or represented by proxy\n1182: at the special meeting and entitled to vote thereon.\n1183: ​\n1184: Q:\n1185: Are there any voting agreements with existing stockholders?\n1186: ​\n1187: A:\n1188: In connection with the merger agreement, the Company, Parent and Longview entered into the voting agreement, pursuant to which Longview has agreed, among other things and subject to certain\n1189: conditions, to vote 7,424,591 shares of common stock in favor of the merger proposal and against any proposal made in opposition to or in competition with the merger or the merger agreement\n1190: that is not approved by the board.\n1191: ​\n1192: As of the close of business on the record date, 7,424,591 shares of common stock represented approximately 7.5% of the total outstanding common stock on such date.\n1193: Q:\n1194: What is a quorum?\n1195: ​\n1196: A:\n1197: A quorum will be present if holders of a majority of the shares of common stock outstanding as of the close of business on the record date are present in person or represented by proxy at\n1198: the special meeting. If a quorum is not present at the special meeting, the special meeting may be adjourned or postponed from time to time until a quorum is obtained.\n1199: ​\n1200: If you submit a proxy but fail to provide voting instructions or abstain on any of the proposals listed on the proxy card, your shares will be counted for purpose of determining whether a\n1201: quorum is present at the special meeting.\n1202: If your shares are held in “street name” by your broker, bank or other nominee and you do not tell the nominee how to vote your shares, these shares will not be counted for purposes of\n1203: determining whether a quorum is present for the transaction of business at the special meeting.\n1204: Q:\n1205: How does the board recommend that I vote?\n1206: ​\n1207: A:\n1208: The board unanimously recommends that our stockholders vote\n1209: “FOR”\n1210:  the merger proposal. The board also unanimously recommends that our stockholders vote\n1211: “FOR”\n1212:  the compensation proposal and\n1213: “FOR”\n1214:  the adjournment proposal.\n1215: ​\n1216: Q:\n1217: What effects will the merger have on PetSmart?\n1218: ​\n1219: A:\n1220: Our common stock is currently registered under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, as amended, referred to as the “Exchange Act,” and is quoted on the NASDAQ Global Select Market, referred\n1221: to as\n1222: ​\n1223: ​\n1224: ~~~ PAGE 12 ~~~\n1225: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1226: the “NASDAQ,” under the symbol “PETM.” As a result of the merger, the Company will cease to be a publicly traded company and will be wholly owned by Parent. Following the consummation of the\n1227: merger, the registration of our common stock and our reporting obligations under the Exchange Act will be terminated. In addition, upon the consummation of the merger, our common stock will\n1228: no longer be listed on any stock exchange or quotation system, including the NASDAQ.\n1229: Q:\n1230: What happens if the merger is not consummated?\n1231: ​\n1232: A:\n1233: If the merger agreement is not approved by the Company’s stockholders, or if the merger is not consummated for any other reason, the Company’s stockholders will not receive any payment for\n1234: their shares in connection with the merger. Instead, the Company will remain a public company and shares of our common stock will continue to be listed and traded on the NASDAQ. Under\n1235: specified circumstances, the Company may be required to pay Parent a termination fee of  $255 million and/or reimburse Parent’s and the Buyer Group expenses up to a maximum amount of  $15\n1236: million (with any expense reimbursement payable to Parent and the Buyer Group reducing the amount of any termination fee payable by the Company), or Parent may be required to pay the Company\n1237: a termination fee of  $510 million or reimburse the Company for certain expenses or losses incurred in connection with the transactions contemplated by the merger agreement. See “\n1238: The Merger Agreement — Termination Fees\n1239: ” and “\n1240: The Merger Agreement — Reimbursement of Expenses.\n1241: ”\n1242: ​\n1243: Q:\n1244: What will happen if stockholders do not approve the advisory proposal on executive compensation payable to the Company’s named executive officers in connection with the merger?\n1245: ​\n1246: A:\n1247: The approval of this proposal is not a condition to the completion of the merger. The SEC rules require the Company to seek approval on a non-binding, advisory basis of certain payments that\n1248: will or may be made to the Company’s named executive officers in connection with the merger. The vote on this proposal is an advisory vote and will not be binding on the Company or Parent.\n1249: If the merger agreement is adopted by the stockholders and the merger is completed, the merger-related compensation may be paid to the Company’s named executive officers even if stockholders\n1250: fail to approve this proposal.\n1251: ​\n1252: Q:\n1253: What do I need to do now? How do I vote my shares of common stock?\n1254: ​\n1255: A:\n1256: We urge you to read this proxy statement carefully, including its annexes and the documents referred to as incorporated by reference in this proxy statement, and to consider how the merger\n1257: affects you.\n1258: Your vote is important.\n1259:  If you are a stockholder of record, you can ensure that your shares are voted at the special meeting by submitting your proxy via:\n1260: ​\n1261: •\n1262: mail, using the enclosed postage-paid envelope;\n1263: ​\n1264: •\n1265: telephone, using the toll-free number listed on each proxy card; or\n1266: ​\n1267: •\n1268: the Internet, at the address provided on each proxy card.\n1269: ​\n1270: If you hold your shares in “street name” through a broker, bank or other nominee you should follow the directions provided by your broker, bank or other nominee regarding how to instruct\n1271: your broker, bank or other nominee to vote your shares. Without those instructions, your shares will not be voted, which will have the same effect as voting “\n1272: AGAINST\n1273: ” the merger proposal.\n1274: Q:\n1275: Can I revoke my proxy?\n1276: ​\n1277: Yes. You can revoke your proxy at any time before the vote is taken at the special meeting. If you are a stockholder of record, you may revoke your proxy by notifying the Company’s Corporate\n1278: Secretary in writing at PetSmart, Inc., Attn: Corporate Secretary, 19601 North 27th Avenue, Phoenix, Arizona 85027, or by submitting a new proxy by telephone, the Internet or mail, in each\n1279: case, dated after the date of the proxy being revoked. In addition, you may revoke your proxy by attending the special meeting and voting in person (simply attending the special meeting will\n1280: not cause your proxy to be revoked). Please note that if you hold your shares in “street name” and you have instructed a broker,\n1281: ~~~ PAGE 13 ~~~\n1282: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1283: bank or other nominee to vote your shares, the above-described options for revoking your voting instructions do not apply, and instead you must follow the instructions received from your\n1284: broker, bank or other nominee to revoke your voting instructions.\n1285: Q:\n1286: What happens if I do not vote?\n1287: ​\n1288: A:\n1289: The vote to adopt the merger agreement is based on the total number of shares of common stock outstanding as of the close of business on the record date, not just the shares that are voted.\n1290: If you do not vote, it will have the same effect as a vote “\n1291: AGAINST\n1292: ” the proposal to adopt the merger agreement.\n1293: ​\n1294: Q:\n1295: Will my shares held in “street name” or another form of record ownership be combined for voting purposes with shares I hold of record?\n1296: ​\n1297: A:\n1298: No. Because any shares you may hold in “street name” will be deemed to be held by a different stockholder than any shares you hold of record, any shares so held will not be combined for\n1299: voting purposes with shares you hold of record. Similarly, if you own shares in various registered forms, such as jointly with your spouse, as trustee of a trust or as custodian for a minor,\n1300: you will receive, and will need to sign and return, a separate proxy card for those shares because they are held in a different form of record ownership. Shares held by a corporation or\n1301: business entity must be voted by an authorized officer of the entity. Shares held in an individual retirement account must be voted under the rules governing the account.\n1302: ​\n1303: Q:\n1304: What happens if I sell my shares of common stock before completion of the merger?\n1305: ​\n1306: A:\n1307: If you transfer your shares of common stock, you will have transferred your right to receive the merger consideration in the merger. In order to receive the merger consideration, you must\n1308: hold your shares of common stock through completion of the merger.\n1309: ​\n1310: The record date for stockholders entitled to vote at the special meeting is earlier than the date on which the merger will be consummated. So, if you transfer your shares of common stock\n1311: after the record date but before the special meeting, you will have transferred your right to receive the merger consideration in the merger, but retained the right to vote at the special\n1312: meeting.\n1313: Q:\n1314: Should I send in my stock certificates or other evidence of ownership now?\n1315: ​\n1316: A:\n1317: No, do not send in your certificates now\n1318: . After the merger is completed, if you hold shares represented by certificates, you will receive a letter of transmittal from the paying agent for the merger with detailed written\n1319: instructions for exchanging your shares of common stock for the merger consideration and if you hold book entry shares you will receive a check or wire transfer for the merger consideration\n1320: with respect to such shares. If your shares of common stock are held in “street name” by your broker, bank or other nominee, you may receive instructions from your broker, bank or other\n1321: nominee as to what action, if any, you need to take to effect the surrender of your “street name” shares in exchange for the merger consideration.\n1322: ​\n1323: Q:\n1324: I do not know where my stock certificate is — how will I get the merger consideration for my shares?\n1325: ​\n1326: A:\n1327: If the merger is completed, the transmittal materials you will receive after the completion of the merger will include the procedures that you must follow if you cannot locate your stock\n1328: certificate. This will include an affidavit that you will need to sign attesting to the loss of your stock certificate. You may also be required to provide a customary indemnity agreement in\n1329: order to cover any potential loss.\n1330: ​\n1331: Q:\n1332: Am I entitled to exercise appraisal rights instead of receiving the merger consideration for my shares of common stock?\n1333: ​\n1334: A:\n1335: If you comply with all the requirements of Section 262 of the DGCL (including not voting in favor of the adoption of the merger agreement), you are entitled to have the “fair value” (as\n1336: defined pursuant to Section 262 of the DGCL) of your shares of common stock determined by the Court of Chancery of the State of Delaware and to receive payment based on that valuation\n1337: instead of receiving the merger\n1338: ​\n1339: ​\n1340: ~~~ PAGE 14 ~~~\n1341: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1342: consideration. The ultimate amount you would receive in an appraisal proceeding may be more than, the same as or less than the amount you would have received under the merger agreement. To\n1343: exercise your appraisal rights, you must comply with the requirements of the DGCL. See “\n1344: Rights of Appraisal\n1345: ” and the text of the Delaware appraisal rights statute, Section 262 of the DGCL, which is reproduced in its entirety as Annex C to this proxy statement.\n1346: Q:\n1347: Will I have to pay taxes on the merger consideration I receive?\n1348: ​\n1349: A:\n1350: The receipt of cash in exchange for shares of common stock pursuant to the merger will generally be a taxable transaction for U.S. federal income tax purposes. You should consult your own\n1351: tax advisors regarding the particular tax consequences to you of the exchange of shares of common stock for cash pursuant to the merger in light of your particular circumstances (including\n1352: the application and effect of any state, local or foreign income and other tax laws).\n1353: ​\n1354: Q:\n1355: What does it mean if I get more than one proxy card or voting instruction card?\n1356: ​\n1357: A:\n1358: If your shares are registered differently or are held in more than one account, you will receive more than one proxy card or voting instruction card. Please complete and return all of the\n1359: proxy cards or voting instruction cards you receive (or submit each of your proxies by telephone or the Internet, if available to you) to ensure that all of your shares are voted.\n1360: ​\n1361: Q:\n1362: What is householding and how does it affect me?\n1363: ​\n1364: A:\n1365: The SEC permits companies to send a single set of proxy materials to any household at which two or more stockholders reside, unless contrary instructions have been received, but only if the\n1366: applicable company provides advance notice and follows certain procedures. In such cases, each stockholder continues to receive a separate notice of the meeting and proxy card. Certain\n1367: brokerage firms may have instituted householding for beneficial owners of common stock held through brokerage firms. If your family has multiple accounts holding common stock, you may have\n1368: already received householding notification from your broker. Please contact your broker directly if you have any questions or require additional copies of this proxy statement. The broker\n1369: will arrange for delivery of a separate copy of this proxy statement promptly upon your written or oral request. You may decide at any time to revoke your decision to household, and thereby\n1370: receive multiple copies.\n1371: ​\n1372: Q:\n1373: Who can help answer my other questions?\n1374: ​\n1375: A:\n1376: If you have more questions about the merger, or require assistance in submitting your proxy or voting your shares or need additional copies of the proxy statement or the enclosed proxy card,\n1377: please contact Innisfree M&A Incorporated, which is acting as the proxy solicitation agent for the Company in connection with the merger.\n1378: ​\n1379: Innisfree M&A Incorporated\n1380: 501 Madison Avenue, 20th Floor, New York, NY 10022\n1381: Stockholders may call toll-free: (877) 456-3510\n1382: Banks & Brokers may call collect: (212) 750-5833\n1383: ~~~ PAGE 15 ~~~\n1384: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1385: ​\n1386: CAUTIONARY STATEMENT CONCERNING FORWARD-LOOKING STATEMENTS\n1387: This proxy statement, and the documents incorporated by reference in this proxy statement, include “forward-looking statements” within the meaning of the Private Securities Litigation Reform\n1388: Act of 1995, which are identified by the use of the words “believe,” “expect,” “may,” “could,” “should,” “plan,” “project,” “anticipate,” “intend,” “estimate,” “will,” “contemplate,” “would”\n1389: and similar expressions that contemplate future events. Such forward-looking statements are based on management’s reasonable current assumptions and expectations, including the expected\n1390: completion and timing of the merger and other information relating to the merger. You should be aware that forward-looking statements involve a number of assumptions, risks and uncertainties\n1391: that could cause the actual results to differ materially from such forward-looking statements. Although we believe that the expectations reflected in these forward-looking statements are\n1392: reasonable, we cannot assure you that the actual results or developments we anticipate will be realized, or even if realized, that they will have the expected effects on the business or\n1393: operations of the Company. These forward-looking statements speak only as of the date on which the statements were made and we undertake no obligation to update or revise any forward-looking\n1394: statements made in this proxy statement or elsewhere as a result of new information, future events or otherwise, except as required by law. In addition to other factors and matters contained\n1395: in or incorporated by reference in this document, we believe the following factors could cause actual results to differ materially from those discussed in the forward-looking statements:\n1396: •\n1397: the occurrence of any event, change or other circumstance that could give rise to the termination of the merger agreement;\n1398: ​\n1399: •\n1400: the failure to obtain the required vote of the Company’s stockholders to adopt the merger agreement, the failure to obtain any required regulatory approval, or the failure to satisfy any of\n1401: the other closing conditions to the merger, and any delay in connection with the foregoing;\n1402: ​\n1403: •\n1404: the failure to obtain the necessary financing;\n1405: ​\n1406: •\n1407: risks related to disruption of management’s attention from the Company’s ongoing business operations due to the pendency of the merger;\n1408: ​\n1409: •\n1410: the effect of the announcement of the merger on the ability of the Company to retain and hire key personnel and maintain relationships with its customers, suppliers, and others with whom it\n1411: does business, or on its operating results and business generally;\n1412: ​\n1413: •\n1414: changes in general economic conditions;\n1415: ​\n1416: •\n1417: the outcome of any legal proceedings that have been or may be instituted against the Company and others relating to the merger agreement; and\n1418: ​\n1419: •\n1420: other risks detailed in our filings with the SEC, including our most recent filings on Forms 10-K and 10-Qs. See “\n1421: Where You Can Find Additional Information.\n1422: ”\n1423: ​\n1424: Many of the factors that will determine our future results are beyond our ability to control or predict. In light of the significant uncertainties inherent in the forward-looking statements\n1425: contained herein, readers of this proxy statement should not place undue reliance on forward-looking statements, which reflect management’s views only as of the date hereof. We cannot\n1426: guarantee any future results, levels of activity, performance or achievements.\n1427: ~~~ PAGE 16 ~~~\n1428: TABLE OF CONTENTS\n1429: ​\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1430]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"/tmp/opencode/petsmart/paged.txt\", \"offset\": 1100, \"limit\": 330}"},"time":{"start":1789824966069,"end":1789824966258}}}}
