9 into Round 1 and shifts Round 1 “Opened” off 10/01.\n--- row 3 ---\n  [Q] Q2\n  [Question] Round 1 deadline outcome: Enforced, or Late bids accepted because Bidder 2 raised its indication during the 10/30–11/02 calls after the 10/30 due date?\n  [Recommended answer] Enforced. Bidder 2's raise is dated from 10/30 (not after) and J.P. Morgan's calls sought rationales; the board selected on the bids in hand on 11/03.\n  [Why, with page] “On October 30, six…” and “From October 30 to November 2…” p. 24.\n  [Rows affected] #17, #23 (Rounds sheet Round 1).\n  [What changes if answered differently] If Late bids accepted, Round 1's Deadline outcome changes and Bidder 2's revised row #23 takes Date from 10/31, sorting after the deadline.\n--- row 4 ---\n  [Q] Q3\n  [Question] Round 2 deadline outcomes: 12/05 superseded before arrival (no outcome recorded), 12/10 Extended, 12/12 Enforced — or should 12/05 count as Extended and 12/10 as Enforced (bids were received on it)?\n  [Recommended answer] As recorded. 12/05 never arrived — revised 12/04–12/05 — so no deadline row or outcome; 12/10 was reached and extended to 12/12; 12/12 was reached and signing followed with no further extension.\n  [Why, with page] pp. 24–26; improvement request p. 25.\n  [Rows affected] #28, #29, #36 (Rounds sheet Round 2).\n  [What changes if answered differently] Counting 12/05 gives “Extended; Extended; Enforced”; treating 12/10 as Enforced gives “Enforced; Enforced”.\n--- row 5 ---\n  [Q] Q4\n  [Question] Indication count: six parties submitted indications of interest (p. 24), but the Reasons section counts “first round indications of interest from 5 bidder groups” (p. 27). Six or five?\n  [Recommended answer] Six. The narrative says six and the arithmetic reconciles (15 signers − 6 = 9 non-submitters; 6 − 4 advanced = 2 eliminated); “5 bidder groups” likely counts only priced or credible groups, or omits one submitter.\n  [Why, with page] pp. 24 and 27.\n  [Rows affected] #17, #21, #22 (Rounds 