{"type":"text","timestamp":1789825675584,"sessionID":"ses_f461f6897ffeZJz46cQwZkb8iB","part":{"id":"prt_0b9ec5931001AYcCYnwSzyk0jK_text-0","sessionID":"ses_f461f6897ffeZJz46cQwZkb8iB","messageID":"msg_0b9ec5931001AYcCYnwSzyk0jK","type":"text","text":"Done. The workbook is saved at **`extraction/petsmart.xlsx`** with the four sheets in order — **Deal ledger** (45 events), **Rounds** (2 rounds, process 1), **Questions** (5), **Deal facts** — headers frozen, filters on, wrapped text, real MM/DD/YYYY dates, controlled values only from the instruction.\n\n## The sale in short\n\nPetSmart's board reviewed alternatives through mid-2014 amid activist pressure from JANA Partners (9.9%) and Longview, and after an approach from \"Industry Participant,\" which the board excluded on antitrust grounds. On 08/13/2014 the board decided to explore a sale and announced it on 08/19. In the first week of October, 15 financial buyers signed confidentiality/standstill agreements and received diligence; six submitted non-binding indications of interest by 10/30 (Buyer Group $81–83; Bidder 2 $78→$81–84; another $80–85; three undisclosed). On 11/03 the board advanced the four bidders at or above $80.00 to a final round (eleven out); two of the four then combined as \"Bidder 3\" (unnamed financial bidder 1 was one of them). Final bids on 12/10 were $80.70 (Buyer Group) and $80.35 (Bidder 2), with Bidder 3 giving only a ≤~$78 verbal valuation and no written offer; after the committee demanded improvements by 12/12, Bidder 2 gave its best and final $81.50 and the Buyer Group $83.00. The board approved on 12/13 and the parties signed on 12/14 at $83.00 per share cash (Buyer Group: BC Partners, La Caisse, GIC, StepStone, Longview; Longview rolled 3,012,050 shares). Bidder 2 exits Not selected at signing; the 15 signers are fully accounted for (9 did not submit, 2 dropped, 2 absorbed into Bidder 3, Bidder 2 out, Buyer Group wins).\n\n## Open questions (flagged in the workbook)\n\n- **Q1 – Round map** (rows 8, 9, 13): one process; Round 1 opens at the first-week-of-October NDA wave (no target outreach wave is reported), Round 2 at 11/03, and the 12/12 call treated as a deadline revision. *Recommendation:* as recorded; the alternative is Round 1 opening at the first substantive contacts (late September/early October), which would move rows 8–9 into Round 1.\n- **Q2 – Round 1 deadline** (rows 17, 23): Enforced. *Recommendation:* Enforced — Bidder 2's raise is dated from 10/30, not after; if Late bids accepted, its row dates change.\n- **Q3 – Round 2 deadlines** (rows 28, 29, 36): 12/05 superseded before arrival (no outcome), 12/10 Extended, 12/12 Enforced. *Recommendation:* as recorded.\n- **Q4 – IOI count** (rows 17, 21, 22): six submitters vs. \"5 bidder groups\" in the Reasons section. *Recommendation:* six; the arithmetic (15−6=9; 6−4=2) reconciles, and \"5\" likely counts only priced groups.\n- **Q5 – Bidder 3's oral statement** (row 35): valuation statement, not a Bid. *Recommendation:* keep it in the exit row; if treated as a bid, add a ceiling row (~$78).\n\n## What I could not do\n\n- Party identities beyond the filing's labels: \"Industry Participant,\" \"Bidder 2,\" \"Bidder 3\" and the unnamed finalists stay anonymous; Bidder 3's second member has no disclosed price, so its IOI is folded into the residual cohort (three submitters).\n- The exact day the initial 12/05 bid deadline was communicated is not reported, so it is dated \"by 12/03/2014\"; likewise the October IOI-deadline communication and November events sit at their month midpoints.\n- Bidder 3 never made a priced, whole-company offer I could record — only a valuation ceiling — so no per-share cells exist for it.","time":{"start":1789825669430,"end":1789825675584}}}
